{% doc %}
  Closes a ticket that has sat resolved for seven days, if it is due.

  Checks the elapsed time itself rather than trusting the consumer's
  delay to be exactly seven days, and reschedules when it is early. That
  is deliberate: the delay looks like a number of minutes, but nothing in
  the platform documentation says so outright, and the failure we refuse
  to ship is a ticket closing on a customer hours after it was resolved.
  Under this shape a wrong unit costs extra wake-ups and nothing else.

  A ticket that has moved on is left alone, which covers the case that
  matters most: the customer replied, the reply reopened it, and the
  event that scheduled this is now stale.

  Separate from its consumer so the decision can be exercised without
  waiting a week for an event to fire.

  @param {string} [ticket_id] - The ticket that was marked resolved
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  assign hold_days = 7

  function ticket = 'modules/support/queries/tickets/find', id: ticket_id, number: null
  if ticket == blank
    return null
  endif

  function status = 'modules/support/queries/statuses/find', id: ticket.status_id
  unless status.kind == 'solved'
    return null
  endunless

  if ticket.solved_at == blank
    log ticket_id, type: 'modules/support/commands/tickets/close_if_due: resolved with no solved_at, leaving it open'
    return null
  endif

  assign now_seconds = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign solved_seconds = ticket.solved_at | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign elapsed_days = now_seconds | minus: solved_seconds | divided_by: 86400

  if elapsed_days < hold_days
    log ticket.number, type: 'modules/support/commands/tickets/close_if_due: too early to close, rescheduling'
    assign payload = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "status_id": ticket.status_id }
    function _ = 'modules/core/commands/events/publish', type: 'ticket_solved', object: payload, delay: null, max_attempts: null
    return null
  endif

  function closed = 'modules/support/queries/statuses/find_by_key', key: 'closed'
  if closed == blank
    log 'ERROR: modules/support/commands/tickets/close_if_due found no closed status to move to'
    return null
  endif

  comment
    Keep the original solved_at rather than taking the one the stamps
    helper offers. It stamps both fields with now, which is right for an
    agent closing a ticket that was never resolved first, and wrong here:
    this ticket was resolved a week ago and that is when it was resolved.
    Overwriting it would quietly turn every resolution time into seven
    days once anything reads the field.
  endcomment
  function stamps = 'modules/support/helpers/status/stamps', status: closed
  graphql _ = 'modules/support/tickets/set_status', id: ticket.id, status_id: closed.id, closed_at: stamps.closed_at, solved_at: ticket.solved_at

  comment
    No actor. Nobody did this, the clock did, and the activity log already
    reads a blank actor as "not a person".
  endcomment
  assign activity = { "ticket_id": ticket.id, "actor_id": null, "action": "status_changed", "from_value": status.name, "to_value": closed.name }
  function _ = 'modules/support/commands/activities/create', object: activity

  return true
%}
